      "statement": "Produce a measurable exact proximal selector p satisfying p+eta*gradientV(p)=y, the genuine quantitative quadratic minimum and uniqueness, and fixed-eta nonexpansivity. The literal Gaussian gradient evaluator G(s,z)=gradientV(p(s)+sqrt(eta(s))*z) is jointly measurable and obeys input/noise Lipschitz constants 1 and sqrteta for equal eta. Produce an actual Markov kernel K(s)=stdGaussian.map(G(s,-)). This covers the exact evaluator (3.2) and only Lemma4.2 (4.4)-(4.5), with no posterior or finite-query claim.",
      "formula": "F_s(x)=V(x)+\\|x-y_s\\|^2/(2\\eta_s),\\quad p_s+\\eta_s\\nabla V(p_s)=y_s,\\quad G(s,z)=\\nabla V(p_s+\\sqrt{\\eta_s}z),\\quad K_s=G(s,\\cdot)_\\#\\gamma.",
      "steps": [
        {
          "title": "Derive the real gradient estimates",
          "text": "Reuse the actual C2 Hessian-to-gradient and strong-convexity APIs. The source upper bound supplies gradientV Lipschitz1. The convex gradient-step theorem supplies id-gradientV Lipschitz1, and convexity supplies monotonicity. None is a theorem premise.",
          "formula": "\\operatorname{Lip}(\\nabla V)\\le1,\\quad\\operatorname{Lip}(I-\\nabla V)\\le1,\\quad\\langle\\nabla V(x)-\\nabla V(z),x-z\\rangle\\ge0.",
          "lean": "actual_gradient_bounds; QuadraticRegularization; SmoothnessEquivalences; GradientDescentContraction; StrongConvexFirstOrder"
        },
        {
          "title": "Construct the actual pointwise damped map",
          "text": "For each actual positive eta, T_s contracts with its own coefficient eta_s/(1+eta_s)<1. The coefficients may approach one over the parameter space. Banach fixed-point construction is pointwise and makes no uniform-parameter contraction or runtime assumption.",
          "formula": "T_s(x)=(1+\\eta_s)^{-1}[y_s+\\eta_s(x-\\nabla V(x))],\\quad c_s=\\eta_s/(1+\\eta_s)<1.",
          "lean": "parameterized_damped_point; ContractingWith"
        },
        {
          "title": "Obtain joint measurability from actual iterates",
          "text": "Every iterate T_s^n(0) is measurable in s from actual measurable eta/y and the continuous id-minus-gradient map. Pointwise Banach convergence with the actual c_s and measurable-limit theorem give a measurable p. Uniform convergence and an upper eta cap are unnecessary; no measurable selector is supplied.",
          "formula": "p_s=\\lim_{n\\to\\infty}T_s^n(0),\\quad s\\mapsto p_s\\text{ measurable, even when }\\sup_s c_s=1.",
          "lean": "ContractingWith.fixedPoint; tendsto_iterate_fixedPoint; measurable_of_tendsto_metrizable"
        },
        {
          "title": "Recover the exact source equation",
          "text": "Multiply the actual fixed-point equation by positive 1+eta and cancel the scalar inverse. The resulting equality is the literal proximal first-order condition for the source quadratic potential.",
          "formula": "p_s+\\eta_s\\nabla V(p_s)=y_s.",
          "lean": "ContractingWith.fixedPoint_isFixedPt; smul_smul; mul_inv_cancel₀"
        },
        {
          "title": "Prove the genuine minimum and uniqueness",
          "text": "Differentiate the true quadratic regularization. Its gradient vanishes at the constructed p. Existing strong-convexity and first-order bounds give the exact coefficient (kappa^-1+eta^-1)/2. Positivity forces norm(z-p)^2=0 for any competing lower point.",
          "formula": "F_s(p_s)+\\tfrac12(\\kappa^{-1}+\\eta_s^{-1})\\|z-p_s\\|^2\\le F_s(z),\\quad F_s(z)\\le F_s(p_s)\\iff z=p_s.",
          "lean": "actual_quadratic_minimum; HasFDerivAt.norm_sq; QuadraticRegularization; StrongConvexFirstOrder.firstOrder_lower_bound_of_strongConvexOn"
        },
        {
          "title": "Keep nonexpansivity at fixed eta",
          "text": "Subtract the two literal proximal equations only when eta_s=eta_t. Monotonicity bounds the inner product below by the squared proximal distance. Cauchy-Schwarz and positive-norm cancellation give nonexpansivity; a zero-distance branch is handled separately.",
          "formula": "\\eta_s=\\eta_t\\Longrightarrow\\|p_s-p_t\\|^2\\le\\langle y_s-y_t,p_s-p_t\\rangle\\Longrightarrow\\|p_s-p_t\\|\\le\\|y_s-y_t\\|.",
          "lean": "gradient_inner_lower_bound_of_strongConvexOn; real_inner_le_norm; norm_nonneg"
        },
        {
          "title": "Use the literal Gaussian gradient evaluator",
          "text": "The actual measurable p, eta square root and noise coordinate give a jointly measurable input to the continuous gradient. No posterior identification or oracle witness is used.",
          "formula": "G(s,z)=\\nabla V(p_s+\\sqrt{\\eta_s}z),\\quad G\\text{ measurable on }S\\times E.",
          "lean": "Measurable.comp; Measurable.sqrt; Measurable.smul; LipschitzWith.continuous"
        },
        {
          "title": "Prove the exact input and noise constants",
          "text": "Apply the true gradient Lipschitz bound, split the input difference with the triangle inequality and use proximal nonexpansivity at equal eta. The literal square-root coefficient stays outside the noise norm. This supplies both source inequalities (4.4)-(4.5).",
          "formula": "\\eta_s=\\eta_t\\Longrightarrow\\|G(s,z)-G(t,w)\\|\\le\\|y_s-y_t\\|+\\sqrt{\\eta_s}\\|z-w\\|.",
          "lean": "LipschitzWith.dist_le_mul; norm_add_le; norm_smul; Real.sqrt_nonneg"
        },
        {
          "title": "Realize the actual Markov kernel",
          "text": "Take the product of the identity kernel and the constant standard Gaussian kernel, then map it by G. Joint measurability proves it Markov; dirac-product and map-map give the exact pointwise pushforward. This gradient-output law is distinct from the RGO posterior and HMC transitions.",
          "formula": "K=(\\mathrm{id}\\times\\gamma).\\mathrm{map}(G),\\quad K_s=G(s,\\cdot)_\\#\\gamma,\\quad K_s(E)=1.",
          "lean": "Kernel.id; Kernel.const; Kernel.prod; Kernel.IsMarkovKernel.map; Measure.dirac_prod; Measure.map_map"
        }
